# Delete - EdgeQL | Reference
created: undefined, last updated: 2026-06-11, [Read more](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/edgeql/delete)

Every link is associated with a *link deletion policy*.
By default, it isn't possible to delete an object linked to by another.
...
ConstraintViolationError: deletion of default::Hero
(af7076e0-3e98-11ec-abb3-b3435bbe7c7e) is prohibited by link target policy
...
To avoid this behavior, we could update the `Movie.characters` link to use
the `allow` deletion policy.
Copy
...
type Movie {
required title: str { constraint exclusive };
required release_year: int64;
multi characters: Person;
multi characters: Person {
on target delete allow;
};
}
```
### Cascading deletes ​
If a link uses the `delete source` policy, then deleting a *target* of the link will also delete the object that links to it (the *source*).
This behavior can be used to implement cascading deletes; be careful with this power!

# v2.0 - Changelog | Resources
created: 2022-07-28, last updated: 2026-04-29, [Read more](https://docs.geldata.com/resources/changelog/2_x)

### Source deletion policies ​
Add deletion cascade functionality with `on source delete`.
Copy
```
type BlogPost {
property title -> str;
}
type Person {
multi link posts -> BlogPost {
on source delete delete target;
}
}
```
Under this policy, deleting a `User` will unconditionally delete its
`posts` as well.
To avoid deleting a `Post` that is linked to by other schema entities,
append `if orphan`.
Copy
```
type Person {
multi link posts -> BlogPost {
on source delete delete target;
on source delete delete target if orphan;
}
}
```

